 "scenario": {
  "description": "Betrawati stands where the Phalakhu Khola meets the Trishuli on the Nuwakot–Rasuwa district line, and its bridge carried the Pasang Lhamu Highway — the only road into Rasuwa district. At 8:37 am on 26 August 2026 rock and glacier ice broke from the north face of Langtang Lirung and fell about 1,200 m into the Lhende Khola, sending a debris flood down the Bhote Koshi and the Trishuli; it reached Betrawati around 9:20 am by the official account (about 9:00 in another reconstruction), minutes after the first SMS alerts went out, and took the bridge with it. The flood entering this map is modelled, not measured: a 1-D hazard-chain model turns the avalanche's melted ice into a debris flood — about 26 million m³ of debris-laden flow leaving the Lhende, 21 million m³ of it water (Nepal’s Flood Forecasting Division measured about 20 million m³ of excess water far downstream) — and routes it 54 km down the valley on top of the river’s mean August flow (about 622 m³/s here, from 44 years of the Betrawati gauge); this is the median of its 9 runs, peaking at about 13,568 m³/s at the north edge. No gauge measured the peak: the one here (warning level 4.1 m, danger level 5 m) last read 3.55 m and was swept away before the crest arrived. The clock is the event's: the run starts at 09:27, five minutes before the modelled front reaches the north edge. The model puts the front at Betrawati at 09:41 (its runs span 09:31–09:52) — later than reported, and that gap is the model’s error, not the river’s: its valley comes from a 30 m elevation model and its channel roughness is a published boulder-bed law (Ferguson 2007) on an assumed, not surveyed, boulder size. Inside the map the run carries no sediment: it moves the water under one uniform debris-flood friction law with a 25 m/s speed cap. The terrain is Copernicus 30 m radar data, a surface model with canopy and buildings in it. The bare-earth filter only runs on ground flatter than 20°, and 77 % of this domain is steeper: the valley floor is filtered and still a metre or two high, the walls above it are the raw surface model. No photograph is baked in — open satellite imagery of this valley could be redistributed, but composing a cloud-free monsoon mosaic of it was beyond this bake and the ready-made cloud-free mosaics are licensed against redistribution — so the ground is shaded by height instead. The rivers start dry and get five minutes of the modelled baseflow before the front, so almost every drop you see is the modelled flood. Watch which roads go under and in what order, and where the routes turn uphill, and do not read street-level depths.",
